WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.375
COLT MCANLIS: In this episode
of Cloud Performance Atlas,

00:00:02.375 --> 00:00:03.960
I show a fishing
friend all the tools

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.222
he'll need to catch a
big performance problem.

00:00:06.222 --> 00:00:07.680
Will this be the
one that got away?

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.540
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:12.900
So my friend Carter
has built a new website

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:16.230
called Bait &amp; Stitch which helps
fishing enthusiasts connect

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:18.270
with designers to
make custom apparel.

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:20.890
After a few weeks in beta,
Carter asked for some help.

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:22.680
He was noticing that
his request latency

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.740
got significantly slower under
heavy load due to the fact

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:28.740
that new instances took
longer times to start.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.840
Now, typically when I
hear feedback like this

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.220
it means one thing--

00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:34.150
cold boot performance.

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:36.060
See, when a new
app engine instance

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:37.710
is spun up for
the first time, it

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:39.600
has to execute a bunch
of work that only

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.370
occurs during initial boot--

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:42.840
things like starting
the debugger,

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.420
initializing global
variables, or populating

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:46.980
some memcached data.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.100
Performance becomes a concern
here because instances

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:53.550
that are typically created
in response to a request that

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:54.300
comes in.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.760
So if an instance is
booting up, the total time

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:58.701
to respond to that
request is a function

00:00:58.701 --> 00:01:01.200
of the normal work that needed
to be done for that response,

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:04.300
as well as any startup work
that has to be done as well.

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:06.960
Now, to be very,
very, very clear here,

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:09.600
cold boot performance
isn't normally a problem.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:12.050
App Engine startup
time is really fast.

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:13.220
I mean take a look at this.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:16.830
I charted the cold boot time for
basic Hello World Application

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.900
across various instant types.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:22.150
250 milliseconds is actually
pretty fast for an F2 instance

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:22.740
to boot up.

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.115
I mean, that's
technically faster

00:01:24.115 --> 00:01:27.210
than fetching a JavaScript
file for most CDNs on a 4G

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:28.570
connection.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:31.710
Which means GAE by itself
is really good at responding

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:33.690
to instance creation time.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:35.340
However, the users
of Bait &amp; Stitch

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:38.516
were noticing performance issues
because of cold boot time,

00:01:38.516 --> 00:01:40.140
which meant that it
was time for Carter

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.270
and I to sit down and
dig in and figure out

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:43.350
what was going wrong.

00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:47.040
And our first stop was take
a look at the logging data.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.750
See, App Engine does
a really good job

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:51.540
of logging information about
each request that it receives,

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.000
most important of which is how
long it took for the request

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.660
to be handled by the service.

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:58.770
Now, logged items which
correspond to system events

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:00.810
are often logged in blue.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:02.650
Startup time is
one of those items,

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:05.850
so we can click on the log
entry to get more data.

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:07.980
Startup requests will
typically have a header value

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:10.259
of loading requests set to one.

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:11.880
Furthermore, these
log entries are

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.620
flagged with some
extra information

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:15.660
that tells you about the
instance being spun up,

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:18.911
and how it will be a little bit
slower than a typical request.

00:02:18.911 --> 00:02:20.910
Now, looking at the logs
we can see that there's

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:24.720
a group of really fast requests
at the bottom of the set, which

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.720
confirms exactly what we're
expecting-- the loading request

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:29.220
is actually pretty slow.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:30.660
What we didn't
expect, however, is

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:32.700
the batch of calls
in the middle which

00:02:32.700 --> 00:02:34.320
follow the loading request.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:37.410
Those are just as slow, but
sadly, at this point logging

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:39.010
won't give us any
more information.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:40.676
So it's time to switch
over to something

00:02:40.676 --> 00:02:42.000
a little more powerful.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.280
And one of the nice things
about App Engine being

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.200
a platform as a
service is that it's

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.840
got a bunch of nifty other tools
built into it that you don't

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:50.250
have to go generate yourself.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:53.670
One of the more powerful
ones is Stackdriver tracing.

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:56.040
See, in addition
to being logged,

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:58.530
each request that comes
through App Engine standard

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:01.020
gets its RPC call information
tracked and listed

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:02.070
in Stackdriver.

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:05.190
To find loading requests we
need to dig around a bit.

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:07.200
They typically aren't
listed directly,

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.020
but they have a higher response
time than normal requests

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:11.070
that come through.

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.200
To confirm this, you
can select a trace.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.984
If the debuglet started command
is in the trace details,

00:03:15.984 --> 00:03:17.400
then it's what
you're looking for.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:20.279
This block only executes when a
new instance has been spawned.

00:03:20.279 --> 00:03:22.320
So it's a clear sign that
this was a request that

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:24.610
caused a cold boot to occur.

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:27.570
Now, as mentioned, any RPC
calls that your app makes

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:28.590
will be logged here.

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:31.650
And we can see that this app
is touching Memcache, doing

00:03:31.650 --> 00:03:34.584
a fetch, and pushing some
stuff to task queues.

00:03:34.584 --> 00:03:36.750
None of those really look
like a performance problem

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:37.650
at this point.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.570
But what's really
interesting here

00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:41.280
is that there's about
900 milliseconds

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:42.720
before the socket was created--

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.730
to allow a connection
to the instance.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:48.270
This is a rough indicator
of the startup time taken.

00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:50.370
So App Engine's boot
time isn't the problem,

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.890
and none of our RPC
requests are the problem,

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:56.050
but Bait &amp; Stitch's loading
time is still about 1.2 seconds.

00:03:56.050 --> 00:03:56.550
Hmm.

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.720
To get more insight we need
to profile more of our code.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:01.650
See, tracing does a
great job of keeping

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:04.020
a tab of what RPC
function calls take,

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:06.000
but it doesn't track
all custom code that

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:07.770
occurs between those calls.

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:11.250
To do that, we need to leverage
the Stackdriver trace API

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:15.690
that allows us to insert custom
tracing data into our requests.

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:18.720
Carter and I were able to write
a small utility class which

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.480
tracked the start and end
time of various code segments,

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:24.960
and would turn those results
into a JSON string on demand.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:27.480
Adding a little
entry/exit setup,

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:31.770
and we quickly had an API to log
how long a block of code takes.

00:04:31.770 --> 00:04:33.780
Passing that data off
to the Stackdriver API

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:36.750
allowed us to insert custom
tracing data for blocks of code

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:38.922
that weren't previously
being logged.

00:04:38.922 --> 00:04:39.630
Pretty neat, huh.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:42.120
Now with the custom
tracing setup,

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:44.130
we now had a very
clear picture of what

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:46.980
was going wrong in the Bait &amp;
Stitch application's cold boot

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:47.820
time.

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.410
And it wasn't pretty.

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:50.790
But to figure out
that problem we

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.290
needed to dig in
a little deeper,

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:54.115
which is a subject
for a different video.

00:04:54.115 --> 00:04:56.490
If you want to learn more
about tracking down performance

00:04:56.490 --> 00:04:58.781
problems, make sure you check
out the rest of the Cloud

00:04:58.781 --> 00:05:00.100
Performance Atlas videos.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:02.550
And don't forget, when
it comes to performance,

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:04.830
every millisecond counts.

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:08.180
[MUSIC PLAYING]

